# The filedialog scene: the picker's request/result grammar, and the
# capability it hands back (DESIGN.md, File dialogs).
#
# THE ASSERTION GOES ALL THE WAY TO THE BYTES. The design's whole claim
# is that kaya hands over a CAPABILITY and never moves the data, so this
# never asserts that a dialog closed. The guest opens the handle it was
# given, reads the file with ordinary `std::fs`, and writes what it read
# into the status label. The line below therefore fails unless a real
# descriptor came back carrying the real file.
#
# WHAT THIS SCENE UNIQUELY PROVES, beyond the handoff itself: the
# capability SURVIVES CROSSING A THREAD. That is the platform-sensitive
# part — a security-scoped URL on iOS, a content:// URI plus a JNI
# reference on Android — and a handle that only opened on the thread
# that picked it would pass every assertion here if the guest read
# inline. Post ordering and transaction nesting are background.steps's
# job, not repeated here.
#
# THE FILE IS THE GUEST'S OWN, written before anything is shown, at
# <temp>/kaya-picked-<pid>/picked.txt. Guest and interpreter are the
# same process, so they agree on that path with no runner involvement,
# and the pid keeps parallel legs from colliding. The script names only
# the BASENAME, so one file serves five lanes whose temp dirs differ.
expect label#0 "no file"

# ARM THE DIRECTORY FIRST. NSOpenPanel honors `directoryURL` only when
# it is PRESENTED — set on an open panel it is ignored, and the panel
# restores whatever location it used last, which is how one run
# inherited a directory an unrelated binary had left behind.
file_dialog_goto $TMP/kaya-picked-$PID
click button#0

# THE PANEL IS REAL AND CORRECTLY AIMED, both read back from the
# platform rather than stamped: the "where" popup names the directory it
# is actually showing, and the list really contains the file. A panel
# pointed somewhere else, or filtered down to nothing, presents
# perfectly and is useless — these two reads are what catch that.
expect_file_dialog kaya-picked-$PID picked.txt

# Select the row and press Open, for real: AX selection and a real
# press of the panel's own OK button, so the panel's own completion
# runs. Not a synthesized answer.
file_choose picked.txt

# THE HANDLER RETURNED WITHOUT READING. The worker holds the capability,
# has opened and read it OFF the app thread, and parks with the result
# undelivered. A guest that read inline arrives here already showing the
# final text and fails on this line.
expect label#0 "reading"

# THE CLAIM. The app thread must still serve input while a read is
# outstanding — `PickedFile::open` blocks, and a cloud provider may
# download the file before it returns. A guest that did this work ON the
# app thread wedged it, so this click is never processed and EVERY step
# below fails too: the second picker never opens, cancel never lands,
# and the closing accessibility read still says "reading". Measured, by
# building that guest and running it. The cascade is the signature — a
# guest that is merely fast cannot imitate it.
click button#2
expect label#0 "1 picked bytes"

# CANCEL IS THE EMPTY LIST, faithfully — no platform can confirm an
# empty selection, so there is no sentinel. This also proves the dialog
# id RETIRED: a second show would panic if the first were still live.
click button#1
expect_file_dialog kaya-picked-$PID picked.txt
file_choose cancel
expect label#0 "cancelled"

# One REAL-TREE read at the end: every assertion above reads kaya's own
# model, and would pass for an arm that ran and drew nothing.
expect_ax label#0 "label/cancelled"
